{"page": 1, "text": "Visible text of EX-99.1_2026-10-29_q326kbr-exhibit991x10022026.htm, sha256 ab195394c45845e424935d23bc556361cb34a0330889b0423493a6f5050ea7e2.\nEX-99.1 2 q326kbr-exhibit991x10022026.htm EX-99.1\nExhibit 99.1\nKBR Reports Third Quarter Fiscal 2026 Results\nThird Quarter Fiscal 2026 Results (All comparisons against the third quarter fiscal 2025 unless noted.)\n\u2022Revenues of $2.0 billion, up 5% \u2022Net income attributable to KBR of $133 million, up 16%; Operating income of $186 million, down 3% with an Operating income margin of 9.2% \u2022Adjusted EBITDA2 of $268 million, up 12% with an Adjusted EBITDA2 margin of 13.2% \u2022Diluted EPS attributable to KBR of $1.06, up 18% \u2022Adjusted EPS2 of $1.12, up 10% \u2022Bookings and options1 of $2.4 billion with 0.9x book-to-bill1\nThird Quarter YTD Fiscal 2026 Results (All comparisons against the third quarter YTD fiscal 2025 unless noted.)\n\u2022Revenues of $5.9 billion, up 1% \u2022Net income attributable to KBR of $331 million, up 9%; Operating income of $538 million, down 8% with an Operating income margin of 9.1% \u2022Adjusted EBITDA2 of $777 million, up 6% with an Adjusted EBITDA2 margin of 13.1% \u2022Diluted EPS attributable to KBR of $2.60, up 12% \u2022Adjusted EPS2 of $3.06, up 4% \u2022Bookings and options1 of $6.1 billion with 1.0x book-to-bill1\nHOUSTON, TX - October 29, 2026 - KBR, Inc. (NYSE: KBR) today announced its third quarter fiscal 2026 results.\n\u201cWe delivered a strong third quarter, with profitable growth and solid execution across both businesses reinforcing our confidence in the full-year outlook,\u201d said Stuart Bradie, President and Chief Executive Officer. \u201cWe also achieved important separation milestones during the quarter, including publication of the Form 10 and the successful implementation of key standalone systems and infrastructure. With leadership teams in place, strong customer demand across both businesses and Investor Days approaching, KBR and Trinzic are well positioned to launch as independent companies in January.\u201d\n1 As used throughout this release, book-to-bill and bookings and options exclude long-term UK PFIs. 2 As used throughout this earnings release, Adjusted EBITDA, Adjusted EBITDA margin, Adjusted earnings per share, Adjusted operating cash flow, and Adjusted operating cash conversion are non-GAAP financial measures. All non-GAAP financial measures reflect results from continuing operations. See additional information at the end of this release regarding non-GAAP financial information, including reconciliations to the nearest GAAP measures 1\nSummarized Third Quarter Fiscal 2026 Consolidated Results\nThree months ended Nine months ended\nOctober 2, October 3, October 2, October 3,\nDollars in millions, except share data 2026 2025 2026 2025\nRevenues $ 2,028 $ 1,931 $ 5,935 $ 5,901\nOperating income 186 191 538 587\nNet income attributable to KBR 133 115 331 304\nNet income attributable to KBR from continuing operations 130 116 328 341\nAdjusted EBITDA2 268 240 777 730\nOperating income margin 9.2 % 9.9 % 9.1 % 9.9 %\n"}
{"page": 2, "text": "Adjusted EBITDA2 margin 13.2 % 12.4 % 13.1 % 12.4 %\nEarnings per share:\nDiluted earnings per share attributable to KBR 1.06 0.90 2.60 2.33\nDiluted earnings per share from continuing operations 1.03 0.91 2.57 2.62\nAdjusted earnings per share2 1.12 1.02 3.06 2.93\nCash flows:\nOperating cash flows from continuing operations 171 198 331 506\nAdjusted operating cash flows2 188 198 371 506\nReturn of capital to shareholders:\nPayments to repurchase common stock \u2014 100 29 304\nPayments of dividends to shareholders 21 22 63 63\nOctober 2, January 2,\n2026 2026\nLeverage:\nNet debt3 2,118 2,117\nTTM Adjusted EBITDA2 1,015 968\nNet leverage 2.1x 2.2x\nThird Quarter Fiscal 2026 Consolidated Results Review (All comparisons against the third quarter fiscal 2025 unless noted.)\nRevenues were $2.0 billion, up 5% or $97 million, primarily due to strong execution of engineering and professional services projects within Sustainable Technology Solutions, partially offset by anticipated reductions in NASA programs within Mission Technology Solutions.\nOperating income was $186 million, down 3% or $5 million, as higher gross profit driven by increased revenues was more than offset by increased spin-off costs associated with the planned separation, higher selling, general and administrative expenses and lower equity in earnings from unconsolidated joint ventures.\nNet income attributable to KBR was $133 million, up 16% or $18 million, reflecting strong operating performance, lower interest expense and a lower effective tax rate driven by interest income associated with a discrete tax benefit, partially offset by higher spin-off costs associated with the planned separation.\n1 As used throughout this release, book-to-bill and bookings and options exclude long-term UK PFIs. 2 As used throughout this earnings release, Adjusted EBITDA, Adjusted EBITDA margin, Adjusted earnings per share, Adjusted operating cash flows, and Adjusted operating cash conversion are non-GAAP financial measures. All non-GAAP financial measures reflect results from continuing operations. See additional information at the end of this release regarding non-GAAP financial information, including reconciliations to the nearest GAAP measures. Trailing-twelve months (TTM) Adjusted EBITDA. 3 Net debt refers to total gross debt before unamortized debt issuance costs and discounts, less cash and cash equivalents. 2\nDiluted earnings per share attributable to KBR were $1.06, up 18% or $0.16, in line with increased net income attributable to KBR noted above and lower diluted weighted average common shares outstanding due to open market share repurchases.\nAdjusted EBITDA2 was $268 million, up 12% or $28 million, driven by strong execution across both business segments, including contributions from unconsolidated joint ventures, favorable portfolio mix and lower corporate costs. Adjusted EBITDA2 margin was 13.2%.\n"}
{"page": 3, "text": "Adjusted earnings per share2 were $1.12, up 10% or $0.10, due to the increase in adjusted EBITDA2 noted above, lower below-the-line expenses and lower adjusted weighted average common shares outstanding due to open market share repurchases, partially offset by higher interest expense associated with unconsolidated joint ventures.\nBacklog and options as of the quarter end totaled $23.1 billion. Book-to-bill1 was 0.9x for the quarter. Excluding the NASA insourcing debooking, book-to-bill1 was 1.0x for the quarter.\nSummarized Third Quarter Fiscal 2026 Segment Results\nThree months ended Nine months ended\nOctober 2, October 3, October 2, October 3,\nDollars in millions 2026 2025 2026 2025\nRevenues $ 2,028 $ 1,931 $ 5,935 $ 5,901\nMission Technology Solutions 1,266 1,326 3,870 4,043\nSustainable Technology Solutions 762 605 2,065 1,858\nAdjusted EBITDA2 268 240 777 730\nMission Technology Solutions 145 138 441 413\nSustainable Technology Solutions 147 128 407 397\nCorporate (24) (26) (71) (80)\nAdjusted EBITDA2 margin 13.2 % 12.4 % 13.1 % 12.4 %\nMission Technology Solutions 11.5 % 10.4 % 11.4 % 10.2 %\nSustainable Technology Solutions 19.3 % 21.2 % 19.7 % 21.4 %\nOctober 2, January 2,\n2026 2026\nBacklog 17,718 16,864\nMission Technology Solutions 11,890 12,552\nSustainable Technology Solutions 5,828 4,312\nBacklog and options 23,122 23,211\nMission Technology Solutions 17,294 18,899\nSustainable Technology Solutions 5,828 4,312\n1 As used throughout this release, book-to-bill and bookings and options exclude long-term UK PFIs. 2 As used throughout this earnings release, Adjusted EBITDA, Adjusted EBITDA margin, Adjusted earnings per share, Adjusted operating cash flows, and Adjusted operating cash conversion are non-GAAP financial measures. All non-GAAP financial measures reflect results from continuing operations. See additional information at the end of this release regarding non-GAAP financial information, including reconciliations to the nearest GAAP measures. Trailing-twelve months (TTM) Adjusted EBITDA. 3 Net debt refers to total gross debt before unamortized debt issuance costs and discounts, less cash and cash equivalents. 3\nThird Quarter Fiscal 2026 Segment Results Review (All comparisons against the third quarter fiscal 2025 unless noted.)\nMission Technology Solutions (MTS) Revenues were $1.3 billion, down 5% or $60 million, primarily due to lower U.S. Government Federal Civilian customer volumes associated with anticipated NASA headwinds, partially offset by growth across Defense & Intelligence and International Government customers.\nOperating income was $115 million, up 2% or $2 million, reflecting higher equity in earnings from unconsolidated joint ventures, favorable portfolio mix and strong project execution, partially offset by lower revenues and higher selling, general and administrative expenses. Operating income margin was 9.1%.\n"}
{"page": 4, "text": "Adjusted EBITDA2 was $145 million, up 5% or $7 million, reflecting higher unconsolidated joint venture adjusted EBITDA contributions and favorable portfolio mix. Adjusted EBITDA2 margin was 11.5%.\nBacklog and options as of the quarter end totaled $17.3 billion. Book-to-bill1 was 0.6x for the quarter. Excluding the NASA insourcing debooking, book-to-bill\u00b9 was 0.8x for the quarter.\nThe following new business awards were announced: \u2022Re-awarded the $8 billion ceiling Antarctic Science and Engineering Support Contract (ASESC) for the U.S. National Science Foundation, supporting U.S. Antarctic Program stations and research camps under a 20-year single-award IDIQ contract following resolution of the GAO protest. This award is not yet reflected in backlog or book-to-bill. \u2022Awarded the $208 million U.S. Army Tactical Aviation and Ground Munitions (TAGM) task order to provide engineering, technical and programmatic support for precision strike and aviation modernization programs over a five-year period of performance. \u2022Awarded the $60 million NATO PATRIOT air and missile defense support contract to provide technical and engineering services supporting the PATRIOT system and its integration with the Integrated Battle Command System (IBCS). \u2022Awarded the UK Ministry of Justice Workplace Services Management contract to provide integrated workplace services across the Ministry's estate, extending a longstanding relationship spanning more than eight years. \u2022Awarded a three-year contract to provide radio frequency engineering and modernization services for a U.S. government customer supporting mission-critical national security systems.\nDuring the quarter, we reached a settlement agreement with U.S. TRANSCOM related to the Global Household Goods Contract, resolving outstanding matters and converting the government's prior termination for default to termination for convenience.\n1 As used throughout this release, book-to-bill and bookings and options exclude long-term UK PFIs. 2 As used throughout this earnings release, Adjusted EBITDA, Adjusted EBITDA margin, Adjusted earnings per share, Adjusted operating cash flows, and Adjusted operating cash conversion are non-GAAP financial measures. All non-GAAP financial measures reflect results from continuing operations. See additional information at the end of this release regarding non-GAAP financial information, including reconciliations to the nearest GAAP measures. Trailing-twelve months (TTM) Adjusted EBITDA. 3 Net debt refers to total gross debt before unamortized debt issuance costs and discounts, less cash and cash equivalents. 4\nSustainable Technology Solutions (STS) Revenues were $762 million, up 26% or $157 million, driven by the conversion of strong bookings secured over the past several quarters into revenue growth.\n"}
{"page": 5, "text": "Operating income was $124 million, up 4% or $5 million, reflecting favorable project mix and execution during the quarter, partially offset by lower equity in earnings from unconsolidated joint ventures. Operating income margin was 16.3%.\nAdjusted EBITDA2 was $147 million, up 15% or $19 million, reflecting strong project execution and favorable portfolio mix. Adjusted EBITDA2 margin was 19.3%.\nBacklog as of the quarter end reached a record $5.8 billion. Book-to-bill1 was 1.3x for the quarter.\nThe following new business awards were announced: \u2022Awarded an offshore engineering contract by Aramco to provide engineering and project execution services supporting upgrades to processing, gas compression and power infrastructure across the Marjan field in the Arabian Gulf. \u2022KBR\u2019s Purifier\u00ae ammonia technology selected for SABIC Agri-Nutrients\u2019 new 3,500 MTPD ammonia plant in Jubail, Saudi Arabia, including basic engineering design, proprietary equipment and catalyst supply. \u2022Awarded the Live Oak e-NG project FEED contract by the Live Oak consortium to provide front-end engineering design services for a proposed large-scale electric natural gas (e-methane) production facility in Nebraska. \u2022Awarded the Kazakhstan Sustainable Aviation Fuel (SAF) project contract by KMG-Aero and KazFoodProducts to license KBR's PureSAF\u00ae technology and provide engineering design for Kazakhstan's first SAF production plant. \u2022Selected for the Morocco green ammonia project Pre-FEED study by ORNX Green Hydrogen to support development of a world-scale low-cost ammonia facility in the La\u00e2youne region. \u2022Awarded contracts to revamp two ammonia plants in Germany and Austria for AGF Nitrogen, leveraging KBR's proprietary ammonia and KRES technologies to improve energy efficiency, reduce emissions and support long-term fertilizer production in Europe.\nBalance Sheet, Cash Flow, and Capital Deployment Liquidity as of October 2, 2026, totaled approximately $1.1 billion, comprising $625 million in borrowing capacity under the revolving credit facility and $439 million in cash and cash equivalents. Net leverage ratio as of October 2, 2026, was 2.1x.\nOperating cash flows from continuing operations for the quarter were $171 million, down 14% or $27 million. Adjusted operating cash flows2 for the quarter were $188 million, down 5% or $10 million.\nDuring the third quarter, KBR returned $21 million in capital to shareholders in regular dividends.\n"}
{"page": 6, "text": "1 As used throughout this release, book-to-bill and bookings and options exclude long-term UK PFIs. 2 As used throughout this earnings release, Adjusted EBITDA, Adjusted EBITDA margin, Adjusted earnings per share, Adjusted operating cash flows, and Adjusted operating cash conversion are non-GAAP financial measures. All non-GAAP financial measures reflect results from continuing operations. See additional information at the end of this release regarding non-GAAP financial information, including reconciliations to the nearest GAAP measures. Trailing-twelve months (TTM) Adjusted EBITDA. 3 Net debt refers to total gross debt before unamortized debt issuance costs and discounts, less cash and cash equivalents. 5\nReaffirming Fiscal 2026 Guidance KBR reaffirms the following full\u2011year fiscal 2026 outlook for the consolidated company.\nFiscal Year 2026 Guidance\nRevenues $7.90B - $8.36B\nAdjusted EBITDA $980M - $1,040M\nAdjusted EPS $3.87 - $4.22\nAdjusted operating cash flows $560M - $600M\nThe company does not provide reconciliations of Adjusted EBITDA, Adjusted EPS, and Adjusted operating cash flows to the most comparable GAAP financial measures on a forward-looking basis because the company is unable to predict with reasonable certainty the ultimate outcome of legal proceedings, unusual gains and losses, and acquisition-related expenses without unreasonable effort, which could be material to the company\u2019s results computed in accordance with GAAP.\nPlanned Spin-Off of Mission Technology Solutions and Investor Days KBR continues to advance the planned separation of its Mission Technology Solutions business, which will become Trinzic upon completion of the spin-off. The transaction is targeted to be completed on January 4, 2027, subject to final approval by KBR's Board of Directors and other customary conditions, including the effectiveness of the registration statement on Form 10 filed with the SEC on September 30, 2026. Upon completion, KBR and its shareholders are expected to benefit from ownership in two independent public companies, each with enhanced strategic focus, operational flexibility and distinct capital allocation priorities.\nTo provide investors with a deeper look at the strategies, growth opportunities and financial outlooks of both businesses, KBR and Trinzic will host separate Investor Day events in New York City on November 11, 2026 and November 12, 2026, respectively. The events will feature presentations from each company's leadership team and will be webcast live.\nAdditional information regarding the planned spin-off, including registration details for KBR's and Trinzic's Investor Day events, is available on the Investor Relations section of KBR's website at investors.kbr.com/news-and-events/spin-off-information.\n"}
{"page": 7, "text": "Conference Call Details The company will host a conference call to discuss its third quarter fiscal 2026 results on Thursday, October 29, 2026, at 7:30 a.m. Central Time. The conference call will be webcast simultaneously through the Investor Relations section of KBR\u2019s website at investors.kbr.com. A replay of the webcast will be available shortly after the call on KBR\u2019s website via the webcast link here: https://events.q4inc.com/attendee/485891893.\n1 As used throughout this release, book-to-bill and bookings and options exclude long-term UK PFIs. 2 As used throughout this earnings release, Adjusted EBITDA, Adjusted EBITDA margin, Adjusted earnings per share, Adjusted operating cash flows, and Adjusted operating cash conversion are non-GAAP financial measures. All non-GAAP financial measures reflect results from continuing operations. See additional information at the end of this release regarding non-GAAP financial information, including reconciliations to the nearest GAAP measures. Trailing-twelve months (TTM) Adjusted EBITDA. 3 Net debt refers to total gross debt before unamortized debt issuance costs and discounts, less cash and cash equivalents. 6\nAbout KBR KBR is a global, capital-light lifecycle solutions company serving customers in high-complexity industrial, energy and infrastructure markets. Through its advisory, technical, engineering and operating expertise, KBR helps customers shape investments, reduce risk, deploy complex technologies, improve performance and deliver reliable outcomes across the asset lifecycle.\nFollowing the planned separation of the Mission Technology Solutions business, KBR will operate as a focused standalone company with differentiated customer relationships, global execution capabilities and a capital-efficient business model. The company is positioned to benefit from long-term secular growth trends across energy security, energy transition, industrial modernization, and infrastructure investment. KBR's 16,000 employees operate across more than 30 countries.\nVisit www.kbr.com\nAbout Trinzic KBR\u2019s Mission Technology Solutions business is expected to be spun off as an independent public company in January 2027 and will then operate under the new name Trinzic. The name is inspired by the word intrinsic, reflecting the essential capabilities, deep expertise, speed and trusted performance that have defined the business for decades. Trinzic will enter the market as a global company and partner to customers supporting some of the highest priority missions across national security, human performance, global operations and space. Trinzic will launch with more than $5 billion in annual revenue, established partnerships and contracts, 20,000 employees and a global footprint.\nVisit www.trinzic.com\n"}
